*======================================================================
* beep.a99 - a program for the workbench's disk: keys 1-8 play the notes
* C D E F G A B C on voice 1 (SNDTONE), 0 silences it, FCTN 9 (BACK)
* returns to the workbench, FCTN = quits.  It needs nothing but the
* workbench's own KERNEL/EQU: assemble it with A, run it with R.
*======================================================================
* SOUND ON THE TI.  The console has a TMS9919 sound chip with three tone
* voices and a noise voice.  A tone is set by a 10-bit DIVIDER: the chip
* divides its 111,861 Hz clock by that number, so a larger divider is a lower
* note (the A above middle C, 440 Hz, is 254).  SNDTONE takes the voice number
* in R0, the divider in R1 and the volume in R2 (0 loudest, 15 off); SNDOFF
* silences everything.  The kernel's music player (MUPLAY, see docs/MUSIC.md)
* plays whole songs; this program shows the simplest use, one note at a time,
* which is also how sound effects are made.
*
* Keys: the program asks for the next key each frame (TICK) and turns the
* digits 1 to 8 into table lookups.  Note the two ways out: FCTN = (KQUIT)
* sets the kernel's quit flag, FCTN 9 (KBACK) returns to the workbench with
* GOMOD; both silence the chip first, because a voice left on keeps sounding
* after the program is gone.
       COPY "kernel.equ"
       COPY "macros.a99"

ANORM  EQU  (RTEXT*16+RBG)*256
ABAR   EQU  (RBARFG*16+RBARBG)*256

       AORG >C000
       DATA INIT,TICK,INIT,0

INIT   .enter
       LI   R1,ANORM
       BL   @CLS
       CLR  R0
       LI   R1,ABAR
       LI   R2,COLS
       BL   @FILLA
       LI   R0,1
       LI   R1,STITLE
       LI   R2,ABAR
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,2*COLS+2
       LI   R1,SHELP
       LI   R2,ANORM
       BL   @PUTS
       .leave
       RT

TICK   .enter
       MOV  R0,R9
       JEQ  T9
       CI   R9,KQUIT*256
       JNE  T1
       BL   @SNDOFF
       SETO @QUITF
       JMP  T9
T1     CI   R9,KBACK*256
       JNE  T2
       BL   @SNDOFF
       LI   R0,PG_WB
       CLR  R1
       BL   @GOMOD
       JMP  T9
* The digit keys arrive as the ASCII codes of '0' to '8' in the high byte, so
* >3000 is '0' and >3800 is '8'.  The code subtracts >3000 and shifts the byte
* down (SRL R9,8) to get the digit 0 to 8: 0 means 'silence'; 1 to 8 index the
* table DIVS, whose entries are words, so the index is turned into a byte
* offset (SLA R9,1: times two) and used as a register-indexed address,
* @DIVS(R9).
T2     CI   R9,>3000           the keys 0-8
       JL   T9
       CI   R9,>3800
       JH   T9
       AI   R9,->3000
       SRL  R9,8
       JEQ  TOFF
       DEC  R9
       SLA  R9,1
       MOV  @DIVS(R9),R1       the divider of the note
       CLR  R0                 voice 1
       CLR  R2                 loudest
       BL   @SNDTONE
       JMP  T9
TOFF   BL   @SNDOFF
T9     .leave
       RT

* The dividers for the C major scale from middle C: 111,861 / 428 is 261 Hz
* (C), 381 is 293 (D), 339 is 330 (E), 320 is 349 (F), 285 is 392 (G), 254 is
* 440 (A), 226 is 495 (B), 214 is 523 (the C above).  The chip's pitches are
* only as exact as the integer divider allows, which is plenty for a scale.
DIVS   DATA 428,381,339,320,285,254,226,214
STITLE STRI ' Beep   a program made on the TI'
SHELP  BYTE SHELPE-SHELPB
SHELPB TEXT '1-8 play C D E F G A B C   0 silence   FCTN 9 back to th'
       TEXT 'e workbench'
SHELPE
       EVEN
